Consider the Saturn system, consisting of the planet Saturn, its ring system, and its moons. The moon Titan dominates the satellite system: the combined mass of all the other mid-sized regular moons (Mimas, Enceladus, Tethys, Dione, Rhea, Iapetus, Hyperion) totals less than 5% of Titan's mass. The ages and orbital histories of Saturn's inner moons and rings remain debated, with various lines of evidence (satellite masses, present-day orbital parameters including eccentricities, inclinations and resonances, ring mass and composition, crater-count surface ages, and tidal/dynamical modeling) pointing to substantial evolution—possibly on geologically recent timescales—of the system.

Determine how Titan formed and how it subsequently evolved within the dynamical history of the Saturn system. Specifically, provide a self-consistent dynamical scenario that explains:
(a) the origin of Titan (e.g., direct accretion in a circumplanetary disk, formation via a giant impact or merger of progenitor moons, or capture);
(b) Titan's role in shaping the present orbital architecture of the inner mid-sized moons and the ring system, including the apparent dichotomy between the single dominant moon and the much less massive inner satellites;
(c) the ages of the rings and inner moons compatible with observed crater statistics, ring mass, and tidal migration rates;
(d) Titan's own current orbital parameters (semi-major axis, eccentricity, obliquity) as outcomes of this history;
all consistent with constraints from measured satellite masses, orbital elements, ring properties, crater-count ages, and improved tidal/dynamical models of Saturn-satellite interactions.

